menuconfig MESHTASTIC_GNSS
	bool "Meshtastic GNSS position service"
	depends on GNSS
	select MESHTASTIC_POSITION
	help
	  Subscribe to Zephyr GNSS data and feed Meshtastic Position payloads to
	  the position module (MESHTASTIC_POSITION), which owns the portnum
	  handling, replies, and transmission.

if MESHTASTIC_GNSS

config MESHTASTIC_GNSS_AUTO_SEND
	bool "Automatically send GNSS fixes"
	default y
	help
	  Send a Position payload whenever a valid GNSS fix is published and
	  the configured interval has elapsed.

config MESHTASTIC_GNSS_SEND_INTERVAL_SEC
	int "GNSS position send interval in seconds"
	default 300
	range 5 86400
	help
	  Minimum interval between successful automatic Position packets.

config MESHTASTIC_GNSS_RETRY_INTERVAL_SEC
	int "Minimum seconds between failed position send attempts"
	default 60
	range 5 3600
	help
	  After a failed automatic position transmission, wait at least this
	  long before trying again. Prevents hammering the radio when the GNSS
	  driver publishes fixes frequently.

if MESHTASTIC_GNSS_AUTO_SEND

config MESHTASTIC_GNSS_SEND_WORK_STACK_SIZE
	int "GNSS position send workqueue stack size"
	default 2048
	help
	  Stack for the dedicated workqueue that sends Position packets
	  (nanopb encoding and PSA AES-CTR on a full payload buffer).

config MESHTASTIC_GNSS_SEND_WORK_PRIORITY
	int "GNSS position send workqueue priority"
	default 8
	help
	  Priority for deferred GNSS position transmissions.

endif # MESHTASTIC_GNSS_AUTO_SEND

config MESHTASTIC_GNSS_TIME_DEBUG
	bool "Log GNSS time-delivery diagnostics (bench only)"
	default n
	help
	  Log one line per GNSS data callback, stamped with k_cycle_get_32() and
	  k_uptime_get() taken at the very top of the callback — BEFORE the no-fix
	  gate, so a module that is tracking satellites but has not yet fixed is
	  still visible.

	  This measures the term that actually bounds GNSS time accuracy, which is
	  not sub-second resolution but one-sided LATENCY: the delay from the true
	  UTC second to the moment we are handed the sentence. That delay is the
	  receiver's own fix latency plus the whole sentence's UART time (~73 ms for
	  a 70-character RMC at 9600 baud, because modem_chat only delivers on a
	  complete match). It is a late bias of order 100-500 ms, not a symmetric
	  quantisation, so it cannot be averaged away.

	  The cycle stamp shares a counter with the board-level `gps pps` edge
	  capture, so an interleaved console capture yields the latency directly by
	  subtraction. Without a PPS reference the same log still yields the
	  inter-callback jitter and the reported sub-second digits.

	  Bench diagnostic. Off in shipping images: it logs at up to the GNSS
	  publish rate.

config MESHTASTIC_GNSS_PPS_AUTOSTART
	bool "Arm the board's 1PPS edge counter at boot"
	depends on SHELL
	default y if MESHTASTIC_GNSS_TIME_DEBUG
	help
	  Arm the board-level 1PPS edge capture during init instead of waiting for
	  `gps pps start` on the console. Only the Heltec V4/R8 implement it; it is a
	  no-op elsewhere.

	  Depends on SHELL because the counter lives in the board's `gps` CLI and
	  because reading the result back — `gps pps`, or `logring` for the captured
	  stream — needs a shell either way. Stated as a dependency rather than left
	  as a silent no-op.

	  This exists because the measurement it feeds happens where the console is
	  not: a receiver needs sky, and a node at a window is a node on battery with
	  nobody typing at it. Armed by hand, a trip to the window collects nothing —
	  which is exactly how the first one went.

	  With MESHTASTIC_GNSS_TIME_DEBUG also on, the per-edge log line and the
	  per-callback line share a cycle counter AND both land in the retained log
	  ring (MESHTASTIC_LOGRING), so an unattended session leaves behind the
	  interleaved PPS/NMEA stream that the latency measurement is a subtraction
	  over. Read it back with `logring` — or `logring prev` if the node rebooted
	  on the way home. The ring is small, so it holds the last tens of seconds,
	  not the whole trip; a handful of paired lines is all the measurement needs.

	  Retained RAM survives a warm reset but NOT a power cycle. A node whose
	  battery died on the windowsill comes back with nothing.

endif # MESHTASTIC_GNSS
